ons, and integrations that leverage agents as a core part of the solution, where it genuinely helps the client. Work closely with VI's UX, consulting, and cloud-native engineering teams to transition prototypes into scalable, supportable production systems. Navigate client infrastructure, security constraints, and existing systems (cloud platforms, APIs, legacy tools) pragmatically. Act as a trusted technical voice in client conversations — you can whiteboard an architecture and defend it in the same meeting. Travel to client sites as needed (frequency depends on engagement). What we expect: Strong full-stack or cloud-native engineering experience, with a track record of shipping real software, not just prototypes that die in a demo. Comfort working directly with non-technical stakeholders and translating their problems into technical solutions on the fly. Solid grounding in at least one modern backend stack and one frontend stack, plus working knowledge of cloud platforms (AWS, Azure, or GCP). Experience with APIs, data integration, and automation — you can connect systems that were never designed to talk to each other. Real, well-founded knowledge of agentic engineering — you understand the underlying concepts (agent planning, tool use, context management, orchestration, evaluation), not just the tool names, and you've applied that thinking to real work, not side projects. A bias toward action: you'd rather build a rough version and test it with real users than perfect a spec. Strong communication skills — you can explain a technical trade-off to a client stakeholder without jargon. Comfort with ambiguity and shifting priorities; forward deployed work is inherently unstructured. Willingness to travel to client sites. Experience in an agency, consultancy, or client-facing technical role (nice to have). Familiarity with UX research or design collaboration workflows (nice to have). Exposure to enterprise integration patterns or legacy system modernization (nice to have). Hands-on exper",
