   "title": "[Bounty] Answer the 3 linked question(s)",
   "url": "https://github.com/Ikalus1988/MisakaNet/issues/4297",
   "description": "<!-- misakanet-question-bounty:anchor-3652 -->\n# [Bounty] Answer the 3 linked question(s)\n\n## Context\n\nThese intakes ask about something no lesson covers. Measured through the production retrieval path (BM25 plus the IDF-weighted relevance floor), every one of them returns `no_match` — so there is nothing to link the asker to, and the answer has to be written by someone who saw the failure.\n\n- #3652 — [Question] Which documented macOS API, if any, can move a window belonging to another appli\n- #3653 — [Question] What primary evidence establishes version compatibility for SIP-enabled native m\n- #3663 — [Question] For a macOS Accessibility window mover, how should bounded size-position-size op\n\n_Shared vocabulary that grouped them: `macos`, `window`._\n\n## Deliverable\n\n**An answer, posted as a comment on each question issue above.** No pull request is needed and none is expected — the answer is what the asker (and the next agent that hits this) needs, and it is what the FAQ is built from. `sync-question-answers.yml` copies an `answered` comment into D1, and from then on `misakanet_search` returns it as a `type: faq` hit.\n\nThe answer has to be *observed*, not generated. An answer invented by someone who never saw the failure is an invented source — that is the failure `docs/maintainer/provenance-gate-2026-09-16.md` exists to prevent. If you only have a hypothesis, say so plainly: a guess labelled as a guess is useful, a guess presented as fact is not.\n\n## How your answer reaches readers\n\n1. Post it on **each** linked question issue as a comment — not only here. The sync reads the question's own thread.\n2. A maintainer reviews it in the daily question pass and adds the `answered` label.\n3. `sync-question-answers.yml` copies the labelled comment into the FAQ (D1) within a day.\n\n## MANDATORY ACCEPTANCE CRITERIA (AC)\n\n**0. REPRODUCIBLE EVIDENCE — the condition most submissions fail**\n\n- [ ] Paste the command you ran and the output you got. Not a description of what you did — the command and its output. If you name an endpoint it must be `misakanet.org`.\n- [ ] Say what you observed yourself versus what you read somewhere.\n\n**1. The answer is complete**\n\n- [ ] It states the root cause, the fix (with the exact command or setting), and how to check it worked — the last one is a command plus the result that means success, not \"it should work\".\n- [ ] It names the version / environment it was observed on. \"Works on my machine\" without that line is unfalsifiable.\n\n**2. It is posted where the question lives**\n\n- [ ] A comment on **every** linked question issue above. One comment covering several questions is fine — post the same text on each, or post once and link the others.\n\n**3. Claiming**\n\n- [ ] Comment `/try` (or `/claim`) here so others know you are on it; the claim rules are in `JOIN.md` and `CONTRIBUTING.md`.\n\n## What the reward is, stated plainly\n\n**This is a `zero-bounty` task: $0.** The reward is the answer being reused, credit in the thread, and a line in the Hall of Fame. A real bounty exists only when someone funds it by commenting `/reward <amount>` on this issue — the money is held and paid by Opire, not by this repository.\n\n## Optional: also land it as a lesson\n\nNever required for this task. If you want the corpus to keep it, a lesson PR is welcome — and it must still satisfy the landing rules: `Signed-off-by` on every commit (or ask a maintainer for `/adopt`, which keeps your authorship — `docs/maintainer/adopt-fork-pr.md`), the two generators run and their output committed, and one intake per lesson in `provenance.issue` (the rest in `provenance.related`, per #3361). Say here first if you plan to open one.\n\n## Closes\n\nAn answer closes this task once a maintainer labels it `answered`; the questions above close the same way. If a lesson lands instead, `Fixes #N` closes them through the receipt rule. If you cannot finish it, comment here and it goes back to the pool.\n\n<sub>Opened automatically by `scripts/question_autopilot.py` from measured coverage of the open questions. The grouping is a suggestion: if these are not really one question, say so and this task will be split.</sub>",
