   "id": 69,
   "uid": "gh_5382506270",
   "source": "github_bounty",
   "title": "[bug bounty] Python wire client fails on valid responses split across stream reads",
   "url": "https://github.com/Tarsnap/kivaloo/issues/358",
   "description": "## Python client fails on a valid response split across stream reads\n\nAt `3de151b5d878b0714552c3658562eb5a87b378ce` (`master`), `tests/python/kivaloo/wire.py:159` passes one `socket.recv(MAX_RESPONSE_BYTES)` result directly to `split_packet()`. The AF_UNIX stream can return fewer bytes than a complete packet. A valid response can therefore raise an uncaught `struct.error` in the Python client even though the remaining bytes are still arriving.\n\nThis report is limited to the Python client/helper and its callers. I am **not** reporting a crash in the C storage server, a security exploit, or stored-data corruption.\n\n### Reproduction\n\nWith the existing `crcmod` dependency installed, run this from the repository root:\n\n```python\nimport sys\nimport struct\nsys.path.insert(0, 'tests/python')\nfrom kivaloo import wire\n\n# A valid KVLDS PARAMS response: 8-byte payload, 28-byte complete frame.\nrecord = struct.pack('>II', 255, 255)\npacket = wire.make_packet(0, record)\nassert wire.split_packet(packet) == (0, record)\n\nclass ShortReads:\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.data = packet\n    def sendall(self, data):\n        assert wire.split_packet(data) == (0, struct.pack('>I', 0x100))\n    def recv(self, size):\n        size = min(size, 8)\n        result, self.data = self.data[:size], self.data[size:]\n        return result\n    def close(self):\n        pass\n\nclient = wire.Wire.__new__(wire.Wire)\nclient.msgnum = 0\nclient.sock = ShortReads()\nprint(client.send_recv('>I', 0x100).get('>II'))\n```\n\nExpected: `(255, 255)`.\n\nActual on the base commit:\n\n```text\nstruct.error: unpack_from requires a buffer of at least 16 bytes for\nunpacking 16 bytes at offset 0 (actual buffer size is 8)\n```\n\nI also reproduced the failure using a real local `socket.socketpair()`, with an event coordinating the second fragment only after the first `recv()` returns. This needs no timing sleeps or remote service. An additional check used the compiled C `lbs` server, private temporary storage and a Unix socket: the unchanged response stream succeeds with the original client on the normal path, fails with the original client when reads are limited to eight bytes, and passes PARAMS/APPEND/GET with byte-exact data after the fix.\n\n### Proposed fix and validation\n\nRead the 16-byte header completely, validate its checksum and the existing total response-size limit, then read the payload and four-byte trailer completely before calling the existing `split_packet()`. Treat EOF before a full frame as an error rather than looping. Keep the existing record-checksum and response-ID checks.\n\nA local minimal patch adds 20 lines and removes two in `wire.py`. Thirteen regression tests pass after the patch, including all 27 split points of the PARAMS response, single-byte reads, complete-response controls, early EOF, size limits, checksum failures, consecutive frames and the real socket-pair reproduction. These tests fail on the unmodified base at the framing cases. Validation used macOS arm64, Python 3.14.7 and the actual `crcmod==1.7` package. The C programs compiled successfully with the documented Homebrew OpenSSL flags; AWS/remote-service tests were not run.\n\nPlease consider this report under the published bug-bounty program if eligible. I understand that classification and any award are your decision, and that awards below $100 are account credits rather than cash. No award is assumed.\n\n**AI disclosure and follow-up:** This report, investigation and proposed patch were prepared by OpenAI ChatGPT/Codex (LLMs), with the authorization of the `avs-io` account owner. This is not intended as a one-off report: questions and requested revisions can be addressed through this GitHub account. No live customer data, external service testing or payment credentials were used.\n",
