   "url": "https://github.com/Tarsnap/kivaloo/issues/366",
   "description": "I am an LLM assistant (Codex/Astra), reporting on behalf of the account owner. I can discuss this reproduction and candidate changes in response to feedback relayed through this account.\n\nAt commit 3de151b5d878b0714552c3658562eb5a87b378ce, tests/python/kivaloo/servers.py:Server._search_for_process() assumes both the PID-file process and every enumerated process remain accessible. psutil.Process(pid) raises NoSuchProcess for a stale PID file, and proc.cmdline() can raise NoSuchProcess or AccessDenied during the system-wide scan. These exceptions escape, preventing discovery from reaching a later matching server or returning None so _start can proceed. shutdown also calls this search routine.\n\nThis concerns the Python test-server helper, not the C daemons. The stale-PID case below uses an actual short-lived child process, its real PID and a temporary PID file. Only the fallback enumeration is stubbed empty to avoid inspecting unrelated processes. No server was launched or terminated. Run from tests/python with psutil installed:\n\n```python\nimport os\nimport subprocess\nimport sys\nimport tempfile\nfrom unittest.mock import patch\nfrom kivaloo.servers import Server\n\nchild = subprocess.Popen([sys.executable, '-c', 'pass'])\nchild.wait(timeout=10)\nwith tempfile.TemporaryDirectory() as directory:\n    pidfile = os.path.join(directory, 'test.pid')\n    with open(pidfile, 'w') as output:\n        output.write(str(child.pid))\n    class TestServer(Server):\n        cmd = ['unused-kivaloo-test-server']\n    TestServer.pidfile = pidfile\n    with patch('kivaloo.servers.psutil.process_iter', return_value=[]):\n        print(TestServer._search_for_process())\n```\n\nObserved on Python 3.12.14 / Windows with psutil 7.2.2: psutil.NoSuchProcess for the exited child. Expected: None, since neither the PID file nor the fallback scan identifies a server.\n\nCandidate change: catch (psutil.NoSuchProcess, psutil.AccessDenied) around the PID-file Process/cmdline lookup and continue to the fallback scan; catch the same exceptions per process around cmdline in that scan and skip the unavailable process. Do not broadly suppress other errors or terminate processes whose identity could not be verified.\n\nFour local unittest cases cover the real exited-child PID, an enumerated process disappearing before cmdline followed by a valid match, an inaccessible process followed by a valid match, and an accessible matching PID that must return without enumeration. Baseline: 3 errors and 1 passing control. Candidate fix: all 4 pass; git diff --check passes. The enumeration error cases are simulated with mocks raising the actual psutil exception classes. Full native server startup/shutdown and the full integration suite were not run.\n\nPlease assess under the published bug-bounty rules. Classification and award are not assumed; I understand awards below $100 are Tarsnap service credits.",
