   "id": 28,
   "uid": "gh_5385863597",
   "source": "github_bounty",
   "title": "[bug bounty] Python wire client rejects valid responses larger than 16 KiB (e.g., LBS GET with block size > 16360)",
   "url": "https://github.com/Tarsnap/kivaloo/issues/369",
   "description": "## Python wire client rejects valid responses larger than 16 KiB (e.g., LBS GET with block size > 16360)\n\nAt `3de151b` (`master`), `tests/python/kivaloo/wire.py` defines\n\n    MAX_RESPONSE_BYTES = 16384\n\nwith the comment \"This is a somewhat arbitrary value\", and `Wire.send_recv()` reads the whole response with a single bounded call:\n\n    idnum, record = split_packet(self.sock.recv(MAX_RESPONSE_BYTES))\n\n`split_packet()` then requires the *entire* frame inside that one buffer:\n\n    record, crc = struct.unpack_from('>%dsI' % record_length, packet, offset=16)\n    assert len(packet) == len(record) + 16 + 4\n\nA Wire frame for a record of length `N` is `N + 20` bytes, so any response record longer than 16364 bytes cannot be processed: `recv()` returns at most 16384 bytes and `struct.unpack_from` raises `struct.error`.\n\nThis is separate from #358 (responses fragmented across reads of a small packet). PR #359's stated fix explicitly keeps \"the existing total response-size limit\", so this bug remains even after #359 is merged.\n\n### Valid server responses that exceed the cap\n\n1. **LBS GET with block size > 16360.** The block size is operator-configured via `lbs -b`, validated to `[PROTO_LBS_BLKLEN_MIN, PROTO_LBS_BLKLEN_MAX]` = `[512, 131072]` (`lbs/main.c`, `lib/proto_lbs/proto_lbs.h`). An LBS GET response record is `4 (status) + blklen` bytes (`lib/proto_lbs/proto_lbs_server.c`, `proto_lbs_response_get()`). Any deployment configured with block size ≥ 16361 — legal up to the documented maximum of 131072 — produces GET responses the Python client cannot read. The C server imposes no such limit: `proto_lbs_request_parse()` `malloc()`s APPEND payloads dynamically and `wire_writepacket_getbuf()` sizes GET responses to the configured block size.\n\n2. **KVLDS RANGE responses.** The response record grows with the data returned: `4 (status) + 4 (num_pairs) + nextkey + Σ(1+keylen + 1+valen)` (each pair up to 514 bytes). For 100-byte keys/values, a range response with roughly 80 pairs already exceeds the cap; `tests/python/dump_kvlds.py` requests `maxsize = 8192` pairs per range call, so the dump tool fails on most non-trivial datasets. The C-side range response is likewise dynamically sized.\n\n### Reproduction (real C server, no mocks)\n\nOn `3de151b`, with `crcmod` installed:\n\n    $ ./lbs/lbs -s /tmp/repro/lbs.sock -d /tmp/repro/storage -b 32768 -1 &\n    $ python3 - <<'EOF'\n    import sys\n    sys.path.insert(0, 'tests/python')\n    from kivaloo import proto_lbs\n    p = proto_lbs.Proto_lbs('/tmp/repro/lbs.sock')\n    print(\"params():\", p.params())\n    data = bytes(range(256)) * 128          # one 32 KiB block\n    print(\"append():\", p.append(1, 0, data))\n    print(\"get():\", p.get(0))\n    EOF\n\n    params(): (32768, 0)\n    append(): 0 1\n    get() FAILED -> error: unpack_from requires a buffer of at least 32792 bytes\n        for unpacking 32776 bytes at offset 16 (actual buffer size is 16384)\n\nThe C `lbs` server accepts the 32 KiB block and serves it (no server-side error); only the Python client fails.\n\n### Impact\n\n- The Python client cannot interoperate with any LBS deployment configured with block size > 16360 (legal up to 131072).\n- `dump_kvlds.py` and any KVLDS RANGE consumer fail once a range response exceeds 16 KiB.\n- The test suite cannot exercise the C server with non-default (larger) block sizes, because the harness itself rejects the responses.\n\n### Suggested fix\n\nIn `send_recv()`: read the 16-byte header first (as #359 proposes), then read exactly `record_length + 4` further bytes based on the header's `record_length` field before calling `split_packet()`. This bounds the allocation by the protocol maximum (LBS: 20 + 4 + 131072 = 131096 bytes; KVLDS: bounded by the requested data) instead of a fixed 16384. `MAX_RESPONSE_BYTES` can then be removed or raised to the protocol maximum.\n\n### Scope / disclosure\n\nThis report is limited to the Python client/helper and its callers (`tests/python/kivaloo/wire.py`). I am not reporting a crash in the C storage server, a security exploit, or stored-data corruption. The C client (`lib/wire/wire_readpacket.c`) reads complete packets and is unaffected.\n\nThis report, investigation and reproduction were prepared by an LLM with the authorization of the `zhangb06` account owner. I am available to discuss the problem or a fix through this account. No live customer data, external service testing, or payment credentials were used.\n",
