   "title": "What are the best-practice architectural workflows for LLM-based contract compliance agents?",
   "url": "https://stackoverflow.com/questions/79907967/what-are-the-best-practice-architectural-workflows-for-llm-based-contract-compli",
   "description": "I am designing an Agentic Workflow for automated contract compliance auditing, where the system must cross-reference multi-page legal documents against a set of compliance policies (e.g., GDPR or local labor laws). My current high-level flow is: Parsing & Indexing: Splitting documents by clause headers to maintain semantic integrity. Retrieval (RAG): Using a vector store to retrieve clauses relevant to specific compliance policies. Reasoning/Evaluation: A dual-agent approach where one agent performs &quot;extraction&quot; and a second agent performs &quot;compliance judgment.&quot; My core architectural concerns are: Handling Cross-References: Many contract clauses refer to other sections (e.g., &quot;See Section 5.1 for liability limitations&quot;). How can the agent effectively maintain this dependency chain without overloading the context window? Decision Consistency: How do you structure the workflow to ensure that the &quot;reasoning&quot; behind a compliance judgment is explainable and verifiable? Agentic Loops: Is it better to use a single &quot;Chain of Thought&quot; prompt for complex compliance logic, or is an autonomous multi-agent framework (like LangGraph or CrewAI) overkill for this specific task? I'm looking for advice or established patterns on how to handle these complexities. What flow have you found most reliable in terms of accuracy and latency for legal-tech applications?",
