   "id": 68,
   "uid": "hn_50043735",
   "source": "hn",
   "title": "Show HN: Korosta – Remember and highlight job postings you've seen",
   "url": "https://chromewebstore.google.com/detail/korosta/dhpnogbefakbfmbihmjkgicbelndidif",
   "description": "# What is and whats not Korosta is a privacy-first user-customizable tool to highlight job postings based on how frequent are those, and your ratings: * how often the job postings appeared to the user -&gt; fade repeatedly seen job postings * learns from your ratings with a simple local model -&gt; highlight relevant ones * 2 versions: free and pro * almost everything is kept local (ratings, recipes, customizations, history), only licensing and payments require a server * no account required to use it (just when doing the purchase) This is not a job tracker, job board, or automated application system. It just annotates what you already have visible in your browser&#x27;s tab. ## Links chrome: https:&#x2F;&#x2F;chromewebstore.google.com&#x2F;detail&#x2F;korosta&#x2F;dhpnogbefak... firefox: https:&#x2F;&#x2F;addons.mozilla.org&#x2F;en-US&#x2F;firefox&#x2F;addon&#x2F;korosta&#x2F; Howto create recipes fast (With the help of a LLM): https:&#x2F;&#x2F;covatools.com&#x2F;korosta&#x2F;howtos&#x2F;create-cookbook-with-an... Website (with ultra short videos): https:&#x2F;&#x2F;korosta.app # Why This idea started like 2 years ago, when I was laid off from a tech company (2024&#x27;s common rounds), and I started the stressful job searching. For job searching, my main platform was, and still is, linkedin: I perfected my url parameters so I can easily have my 10 different tabs. But one questions that commonly had, was &quot;Have I already seen this job post?&quot;, and honestly, I wont be saving manually every item in a tool like trello (or the new ones). That question plus my bad memory had me with: &quot;that role is awesome!!! ohhh I already seen it... but 2 months ago&quot; (something like job posting recycling? maybe). The main objective of this tool&#x2F;extension is to reduce the load &#x2F; fatigue of remembering what you have already seen (and not in just a single site), and also highlight what could be interesting to you personally (by learning with your ratings) -&gt; less time searching, and thus, giving more time to apply in the relevant ones, or doing something else (like recovering your energy). One thing I also spent time around was in the customization &#x2F; editor (+ the howto in the page), so almost anybody can extend the tool (recipes&#x2F;cookbook) &lt;- I get frustrated when I love a tool but I fully depend on the developer # beyond the technical part (more context... you can skip it, and test already) Around 16.5 years ago I wanted to develop and sell apps, like the trend in those days... I did not. One year ago, I was thinking the same with the first version, the tool was working (even though over engineered), but I got discouraged by myself by reading all the terms of services, legal + fiscal topics, all the things that I did not know... plus some sites are very aggressive with their terms of use and tools like this. I got a job, then changed my career path to a contractor, and meanwhile I started looking for a job again, but since the end of 2025 I really understand the new difficulties of the job market + not being sooo young, so I took the previous tool, refurbished it but with a different mindset: customization first with a simple learning algorithm so it can highlight interesting jobs to the user -&gt; the name of the tool &quot;Korosta&quot; means highlight in Finnish (its difficult to find a good name ha). I do think that is even more relevant to release it now because of AI: some platforms are pushing heavily their AI-based search engines, that are not so easy yet to customize -&gt; putting more stress &#x2F; burden on us. In terms of monetization, I defined it based on: I am not keen of subscriptions, I grew up with shareware (like Doom... you can guess my age now), and when a single payment give you something (I wont distribute this in floppies, though). And it ended like: pay once and keep for the same major version. I learned a lot, and I am still learning, with this development, so I want to hear you",
   "budget_min": null,
   "budget_max": null,
   "currency": "USD",
