   "description": "Context I'm working on an integration project with a wordpress website. I have ssh access to the wordpress server. I'm using fastapi (python framework for web APIs) for the backend of my project. The app I'm developing will be accessible in a sub-domain of the wordpress site to benefit from wordpress authentication via auth logged_in_[hash] cookies. Problem To run my tests, I authenticated to wordpress to retrieve the logged_in_[hash] session cookie. Based on : wp_hash() generate_auth_cookie() wp_salt() Code extract from wordpress : function wp_generate_auth_cookie( $user_id, $expiration, $scheme = 'auth', $token = '' ) { $user = get_userdata( $user_id ); if ( ! $user ) { return ''; } if ( ! $token ) { $manager = WP_Session_Tokens::get_instance( $user_id ); $token = $manager-&gt;create( $expiration ); } $pass_frag = substr( $user-&gt;user_pass, 8, 4 ); $key = wp_hash( $user-&gt;user_login . '|' . $pass_frag . '|' . $expiration . '|' . $token, $scheme ); // If ext/hash is not present, compat.php's hash_hmac() does not support sha256. $algo = function_exists( 'hash' ) ? 'sha256' : 'sha1'; $hash = hash_hmac( $algo, $user-&gt;user_login . '|' . $expiration . '|' . $token, $key ); $cookie = $user-&gt;user_login . '|' . $expiration . '|' . $token . '|' . $hash; I try to create hmac like wordpress and if my generated hmac == hmac_from_cookie it's win. def wp_hash(data: str) -&gt; str: &quot;&quot;&quot;Replicate WordPress's wp_hash function with MD5 HMAC.&quot;&quot;&quot; salt = f&quot;{WP_AUTH_KEY}{WP_LOGGED_IN_SALT}&quot; return hmac.new(salt.encode(), data.encode(), hashlib.md5).hexdigest() def validate_wp_cookie(cookie_value: str) -&gt; bool: cookie_value = urllib.parse.unquote(cookie_value) # %7C -&gt; | parts = cookie_value.split(&quot;|&quot;) if len(parts) != 4: print(&quot;Invalid cookie format.&quot;) return False username, expiration, token, expected_hmac = parts user_pass = get_user_by_username(username).get(&quot;user_pass&quot;) pass_frag = user_pass[8:12] key = wp_hash(f&quot;{username}|{pass_frag}|{expiration}|{token}&quot;) hash_hmac = hmac.new( key.encode(), f&quot;{username}|{expiration}|{token}&quot;.encode(), hashlib.sha256 ).hexdigest() cookie = f&quot;{username}|{expiration}|{token}|{hash_hmac}&quot; cookie = urllib.parse.quote(cookie) print(&quot;expected_hmac : &quot;, expected_hmac) print(&quot;hash_hmac : &quot;, hash_hmac) return hmac.compare_digest(cookie, cookie_value) cookie values are the same for username | expiration | token but different for hmac I've tried with sha1 and sha256, only 256 gives me the right length, so I guess 256 is used. So I tried different keys for wp_hash: def wp_hash(data: str, scheme: str = &quot;auth&quot;) -&gt; str: &quot;&quot;&quot;Replicate WordPress's wp_hash function with MD5 HMAC.&quot;&quot;&quot; salt = f&quot;{WP_AUTH_KEY}&quot; # or f&quot;{WP_SECURE_AUTH_KEY}&quot; or f&quot;{WP_AUTH_KEY}{WP_LOGGED_IN_SALT}&quot; return hmac.new(salt.encode(), data.encode(), hashlib.md5).hexdigest() But nothing works. Do you have any idea how I could get the same hmac? Or if it's possible to check the authenticity of the logged_in_[hash] auth cookie more simply? As a reminder: I have ssh access to the machine that hosts wordpress, and I don't know if there's any link but on wordpress the plugins used for membership management is paid membership pro.",
