   "source": "hn",
   "title": "Show HN: Pointless but mostly-exact clone of Hacker News",
   "url": "https://news.ycombinator.lol",
   "description": "Source code: http:&#x2F;&#x2F;github.com&#x2F;shawwn&#x2F;sharc I had more things I wanted to do with this, but I should probably admit to myself I probably won&#x27;t be. The original idea was for https:&#x2F;&#x2F;news.ycombinator.lol to be &quot;Hacker News, but all the comments are AI.&quot; Right now it&#x27;s &quot;Hacker News, but all the comments are the same as the real HN.&quot; Hence, it&#x27;s completely pointless. Special thanks to dang for collaborating -- he even suggested the name for the codebase, &quot;sharc&quot;. Development time was about four months of near daily work: https:&#x2F;&#x2F;github.com&#x2F;shawwn&#x2F;sharc&#x2F;tree&#x2F;main&#x2F;docs&#x2F;agents&#x2F;handof... Some days I&#x27;d spend a solid 16 hours on this. What&#x27;s the point? None, really. Just to test the limits of my abilities and prove to myself that I could do what pg did. It&#x27;s an extremely efficient port of HN to Common Lisp. One of the project&#x27;s goals was to be high performance. According to benchmarks, it can generate pages very slightly slower than real HN. (dang shared some timings on a particularly busy page, along with how many views it got over a certain time period, for benchmarking throughput. I never got around to verifying whether I can handle 24 million views a day, though, so I&#x27;d imagine something might break if you scale this up. On the other hand, I think it&#x27;ll hold up.) If you&#x27;re particularly offended that this is copying your comments, claim your account by going to your profile (for example, I would go to https:&#x2F;&#x2F;news.ycombinator.lol&#x2F;user?id=sillysaurusx ) and then click &quot;claim&quot; at the bottom. It&#x27;ll ask you to put a 6 digit code into your real HN profile, at which point you&#x27;ll be able to reset your cloned profile&#x27;s password. Log in, go to your profile page, and set &quot;redact&quot; to &quot;yes&quot;, the same way you&#x27;d set &quot;showdead&quot; to &quot;yes&quot; on HN. So, why make this? Eh, call it an art project. That&#x27;s code for &quot;It would&#x27;ve been a lot funnier with AI generated comments, but I seem to have stopped working on this for now.&quot; I&#x27;ll post an update someday, if I can figure out how to do it without paying hundreds of dollars of API token prices. &#x2F;ask and &#x2F;show are missing. Those were the only two features left to clone. But pretty much everything else is there. It&#x27;s been running for a couple months now, so I suppose it&#x27;s stable enough for a public showing. Not that there&#x27;s any reason to look. Shoo! Go find a more worthy project to devote your attention to. :) Kind of melancholy admitting that I spent four months with only something pointless to show for it. But then again, here&#x27;s the punchline: go to any random comment on HN, then in the url change .com to .lol and you&#x27;ll see an almost pixel perfect recreation. For example, https:&#x2F;&#x2F;news.ycombinator.lol&#x2F;item?id=49995615 Sadly you can&#x27;t form a loop, because if you edit your original comment with a link to itself, you&#x27;d only be able to go from HN to .lol to HN. But you could list both and switch between them as much as you want. For example: https:&#x2F;&#x2F;news.ycombinator.lol&#x2F;item?id=49996495 https:&#x2F;&#x2F;news.ycombinator.com&#x2F;item?id=49996495 This project was one of the most technically demanding things I&#x27;ve ever done. People look at HN and go oh, that? That looks easy. If you think so, I invite you to write it yourself -- it&#x27;s an exercise that&#x27;ll certainly make you a better programmer, and gain appreciation for how many subtle features we enjoy here on HN. Ha, and I just discovered a new one. I mixed up the &quot;title&quot; and &quot;url&quot; boxes just now. HN popped up a nice looking overlay that said &quot;please enter a URL&quot;, pointing to the actual url box. https:&#x2F;&#x2F;i.imgur.com&#x2F;agFas7x.png The HN team is always adding something or anothe",
