   "source": "github_bounty",
   "title": "The reward analysis backlog is blocked on a GRPO run that has never happened",
   "url": "https://github.com/marcelo-earth/marcello/issues/37",
   "description": "`outputs/` contains `classifier/` only: `best`, `final`, `judge`, `sanity_probe.json`, `backbone_comparison.json`. There is no `outputs/grpo/`, no `outputs/eval/`, and no completions anywhere in the repo. PLAN.md Steps 4 through 7 are untouched. GRPO has never run.\n\nMeanwhile, four open issues all ask questions that can only be answered by reading completions from a GRPO run:\n\n- #30 is the completion length distribution pinned at `max_completion_length`\n- #15 reward-hacking audit of the top-k completions\n- #18 group reward variance, near-zero spread means no gradient\n- #19 learning rate sweep, 5e-7 may be too low to move the policy\n\nThree separate contributors have now written analysis tooling against this gap. #31 (`audit_rewards.py`, closed by its author) and #33 (`analyze_completion_lengths.py`, open) are both instruments for data that does not exist, and both PR descriptions say so in their own words: \"the actual histogram data needs completions from a real GRPO training\", \"the tool is unexercised on real data\".\n\nThat is not the contributors' fault. The issues read as if the data were sitting there. But the effect is that effort keeps flowing into measuring a run nobody has launched, and none of the four issues can close.\n\n**The actual order**\n\n1. **#12 first, and it is the only issue labelled `blocking` for a reason.** The classifier has never seen a base-model generation, which is the entire distribution it will score during GRPO. Every reward the policy receives passes through it. Running Step 5 before fixing #12 produces a run whose rewards are untrustworthy, which means the #30 and #15 analyses would be measuring an artifact.\n2. **Then Step 5, GRPO from base**, producing `outputs/grpo/` and an `outputs/eval/latest.json` written with `--reward-config` so the per-prompt reward breakdowns exist. Without that flag the breakdown keys are absent (`scripts/evaluate.py:173`) and the audit tooling has nothing to read.\n3. **Then #30, #15, #18, #19**, in whatever order the run's numbers make interesting.\n\n**What to do here**\n\nMake the dependency explicit so contributors are not drawn into building instruments for absent data:\n\n- Note on #30, #15, #18 and #19 that they are gated on a Step 5 run, and that until then only the tool can be delivered, not the finding.\n- Consider a `blocked` label, or milestones, so the gating is visible from the issue list rather than only from reading `outputs/`.\n- Once the run exists, post the artifact paths on each of the four issues so the tooling has a concrete target.\n\nThe project is not short on tooling. It is short on one run.\n",
   "budget_min": null,
   "budget_max": null,
   "currency": "USD",
   "posted_at": "2026-08-12T23:13:32Z",
   "fetched_at": "2026-11-04T04:09:46Z",
