   "title": "[CRITICAL] R0's citation collapse is reward-optimal, not emergent hacking: reframe it",
   "url": "https://github.com/emrsyah/needle/issues/2",
   "description": "**Severity:** critical · **Raised by reviewer(s):** A, B, C\r\n\r\n**Location:** ch3 §3.3–3.4 (R0 definition); ch4 §4.4–4.5; ch5 §5.1; abstract\r\n\r\n### Problem\r\nUnder R0 every weight except EM is 0, but invalid episodes still get `PROTOCOL_REWARD = -1.0` (`src/needle/training/rollouts.py:37`). A citation to an unknown title or sentence raises `SearchEnvironmentError` (`src/needle/environment/search.py:170-180`), which makes the episode invalid. An uncited answer is valid and can earn +1. So under R0 a citation can only cost reward and never earn any, and dropping citations is the reward-maximising policy. B0's main failure mode was exactly this: 85/130 failures were invented citations. The thesis presents the collapse as reward hacking (Skalse) and concludes that \"the shortcut comes from the reward\", which is close to true by construction.\r\n\r\n### Why it matters\r\nEvery reviewer flagged this independently. An examiner will say the headline RQ2 finding is a designed-in incentive.\r\n\r\n### Suggested fix\r\n- Add a short derivation in §3.4 and §4.5: under R0, E[R | cite] ≤ E[R | no cite].\r\n- Reframe as \"outcome reward + citation-validity penalty pushes the policy to omit citations\". The interesting part becomes *timing and seed-dependence* (s7 did not collapse).\r\n- From R0 s8/s9 `metrics.jsonl` rollouts, count the share of invalid rollouts caused by citation errors in steps 1–96 (CPU only).\r\n- List as future work an R0 variant that requires citing but only checks the format.\r\n\r\n---\r\n_From the simulated IEEE peer review of the thesis (3 independent reviewers, merged). Tracked in the summary issue._",
